WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0031: the bridge and the torch,
a junior-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.833
Two parts: four people by hand,
then code that finds the minimum for any number.

3
00:00:10.633 --> 00:00:15.100
Part A: what is the shortest time
for all four to cross the bridge?

4
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:21.100
Four people reach a narrow bridge at night.
Their times: 1, 2, 5 and 10 minutes.

5
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.633
They have one torch, and without it
nobody can cross.

6
00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:28.633
At most two on the bridge at once,
and a pair walks at the slower one's pace.

7
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:32.633
And someone has to bring the torch back
so the others can cross.

8
00:00:32.633 --> 00:00:36.633
The first plan that comes to mind: A, the fastest,
escorts each one and brings the torch back.

9
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:41.733
A and D cross together: 10 minutes,
D's pace.

10
00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:46.833
A returns with the torch: one more minute.

11
00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:51.933
A and C cross: 5 minutes.
16 have passed.

12
00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:57.033
A returns again: one minute.

13
00:00:57.033 --> 00:01:02.133
A and B cross: 2 minutes.
Everyone is across after 19 minutes.

14
00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:06.167
19 looks reasonable: the fastest always returns.
But we can do better.

15
00:01:06.967 --> 00:01:12.000
The problem: this plan pays the 10
and also the 5, in two different crossings.

16
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:17.167
The idea: if C and D cross as one pair,
the 5 is swallowed by the 10, and we pay only 10.

17
00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:22.400
The price: someone fast must already wait
on the far bank to bring the torch back.

18
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.400
So the two fastest go first.
Let's see it on the bridge.

19
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.100
Step 1: A and B cross, 2 minutes.
B will wait on the far bank.

20
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:38.200
Step 2: A returns with the torch, 1 minute.
3 minutes have passed.

21
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:43.300
Step 3: C and D cross together, 10 minutes.
C's time is swallowed. 13 so far.

22
00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:48.400
Step 4: B, who waited, brings the torch back.
2 minutes, 15 so far.

23
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:53.500
Step 5: A and B cross, 2 minutes.
Everyone is across after 17 minutes.

24
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:58.667
17, not 19: instead of the 5 we pay 2 twice
and one more minute, only four minutes.

25
00:01:59.467 --> 00:02:04.967
The page's box compares the plans:
the search, the fastest escorts, and the two slow together.

26
00:02:04.967 --> 00:02:08.967
The search finds 17, like the two slow ones together.
The fastest escorting gives 19.

27
00:02:09.567 --> 00:02:13.733
And it plays the search's plan,
crossing by crossing.

28
00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:16.867
A and B cross, and the torch goes with them.

29
00:02:16.867 --> 00:02:20.000
A brings the torch back.

30
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.133
C and D cross together: t = 13.

31
00:02:23.133 --> 00:02:26.267
B brings the torch back: t = 15.

32
00:02:26.267 --> 00:02:29.667
A and B cross: t = 17,
and everyone is across.

33
00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:35.000
Why is less than 17 impossible? First:
how many crossings are needed at least?

34
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:39.000
Each forward crossing moves at most two:
three forward, and two back between them. Five.

35
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.500
D crosses forward at least once:
10 minutes.

36
00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:47.400
Each of the other four crossings takes
at least the time of its fastest person.

37
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.733
Case 1: C does not cross with D.
Then C crosses on its own once more, 5 at least.

38
00:02:52.733 --> 00:02:57.533
And three more crossings of a minute at least:
10 + 5 + 3 = 18. More than 17.

39
00:02:57.533 --> 00:03:02.533
Case 2: C crosses with D, 10 minutes.
Before that, someone crossed and waited on the far bank:

40
00:03:02.533 --> 00:03:07.633
a forward crossing of 2 at least, since A alone
cannot both wait there and come back.

41
00:03:07.633 --> 00:03:13.133
The torch comes back twice: 1 and 2 at least,
since one person cannot be on both banks.

42
00:03:13.133 --> 00:03:17.733
And the last crossing, 2 at least:
2 + 1 + 10 + 2 + 2 = 17.

43
00:03:17.733 --> 00:03:22.900
In both cases at least 17, and our plan
reaches exactly 17. That is the minimum.

44
00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:29.200
The cost: every plan for four people is
five crossings, and there are only a few dozen plans.

45
00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.000
In part B code will go over all of them,
for any number of people.

46
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:39.000
Part B: a function that takes the times and returns
the shortest time and the crossings.

47
00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:44.300
The state midway: who is already across,
a bit per person, and where the torch is.

48
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:49.333
Here A and B are across, and the torch is there.
What happened before does not change what follows.

49
00:03:49.333 --> 00:03:52.833
With N people there are 2^N · 2 states.
For four, 32.

50
00:03:52.833 --> 00:03:56.833
The time spent is not part of the state:
it is the cost of the path to it.

51
00:03:57.633 --> 00:04:02.367
A crossing is an edge between two states,
and its cost is the slower one's time.

52
00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:07.400
BFS counts edges: both plans
have five, and BFS cannot tell them apart.

53
00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:12.900
With positive costs we need Dijkstra:
always pop the state with the least time.

54
00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:16.700
Now let's write it in Python.

55
00:04:20.067 --> 00:04:24.567
heapq is a heap: the smallest comes out first.
combinations gives all the pairs.

56
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.833
bridge takes the list of times.

57
00:04:31.867 --> 00:04:36.367
n people, and everyone is the mask
with all of them across: 1111.

58
00:04:37.667 --> 00:04:41.633
The start state: nobody has crossed,
and the torch is on the near bank.

59
00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:50.800
best: the best time to each state.
parent: where we came from. The heap holds the start.

60
00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:58.800
While the heap is not empty, pop
the state with the least time.

61
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:05.100
A stale entry, of a state already reached
faster, is thrown away.

62
00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:14.200
If everyone is across, we are done:
the state came out with the least time.

63
00:05:19.067 --> 00:05:23.567
bank: who is on the torch's bank.
Their bit equals the torch's side.

64
00:05:28.833 --> 00:05:32.867
Any group of one or two
from that bank may cross.

65
00:05:39.867 --> 00:05:44.367
The cost: t plus the slowest time in the group.
Their bits flip, and the torch changes side.

66
00:05:53.733 --> 00:05:58.233
If this is a cheaper way to the new state,
update time and parent, and push it.

67
00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:11.733
At the end, walk back from the final state
by the parents, collecting the crossings.

68
00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:19.400
And return the time, and the crossings
in the right order, from the start.

69
00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:25.567
Run it on 1, 2, 5, 10: the variables under the code,
and on the right each state popped from the heap.

70
00:06:25.567 --> 00:06:30.733
n = 4. The start: nobody across,
torch near, time 0, and it is in the heap.

71
00:06:30.733 --> 00:06:36.333
Pop the start, t = 0.
All four are on the torch's bank.

72
00:06:36.333 --> 00:06:41.633
The first group, A alone: cost 1.
Less than infinity, so it is pushed.

73
00:06:41.633 --> 00:06:46.133
So for all ten groups: ten states
enter the heap, each with its own time.

74
00:06:46.133 --> 00:06:51.600
(A, far) pops at time 1. Only A is by the torch,
and going back does not improve the start.

75
00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:55.067
(B, far) at time 2
adds no new state either.

76
00:06:55.067 --> 00:07:00.067
(AB, far) at 2: A goes back, a new state at 3.
B goes back, a new state at 4.

77
00:07:00.067 --> 00:07:04.467
The heap gives the states out by time:
3, 4, 5, 5, 5, 6.

78
00:07:04.467 --> 00:07:09.467
At 9 (BC, near) pops: A and D cross,
and everyone is across for the first time, at 19.

79
00:07:09.467 --> 00:07:13.767
The 19 waits in the heap. First come
all the states from 10 to 15.

80
00:07:13.767 --> 00:07:18.767
(CD, near) at time 15: A and B cross.
15 + 2 = 17, less than 19: update.

81
00:07:18.767 --> 00:07:23.733
The final state pops at 17, before the old 19.
Everyone is across: break.

82
00:07:23.733 --> 00:07:28.333
The walk back by the parents:
five crossings, and 17 is returned.

83
00:07:29.133 --> 00:07:34.633
The parents give the crossings from last to first.
Reversed, they are the 17 plan of part A.

84
00:07:34.633 --> 00:07:37.767
A and B forward: 2 minutes.

85
00:07:37.767 --> 00:07:40.900
A returns with the torch: 3 minutes so far.

86
00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:44.033
C and D forward: 13 minutes so far.

87
00:07:44.033 --> 00:07:47.500
B returns, then A and B forward:
17 minutes in all.

88
00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:53.467
The cost: S = 2^N · 2 states, 512 for eight.
O(N²) groups from each state.

89
00:07:53.467 --> 00:07:57.467
With a heap, time O(S N² log S),
and memory O(S) for the times and parents.

90
00:07:58.267 --> 00:08:01.867
A trap: putting the elapsed time
into the state as well.

91
00:08:01.867 --> 00:08:06.900
Then every state is "new", the best check
prunes nothing, and the search explodes.

92
00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:11.700
The time is the cost of the path, not part
of the state. Then there are only 32 states.

93
00:08:12.495 --> 00:08:18.095
To sum up: the two slow ones cross together, 17 minutes,
and Dijkstra over the states for any number.

94
00:08:18.095 --> 00:08:22.062
Try your own times in the box
on the page of question 0031 on the site.
